Ow ow ow ouch ouch ow.
A week of playing nethack, up in smoke.  I know we've all heard
this story many times, but it scarcely hurts less when it happens
to you again.

What happened was this...

I made a little modification to nethack.  (That's what nethack's
about, right?)  Just a user interface adjustment; nothing that should
have affected the validity of save files.  Then I moved the new
nethack executable into nethackdir.  (I moved the old one to a safe
place first.)  I had meant to move my save file out of the way just in
case, but I forgot.  Then I typed "nethack <RETURN>".  Nethack printed
"Saved level is out of date." and immediately my precious save file
was unlinked, without *any* opportunity for me to prevent the loss.
I really don't believe that the save file was invalid, but even if
it was, I would have liked to take my chances with it!

I earnestly plead that save files not be deleted automatically.
Instead, they could be renamed to <file>.e, as error save files are.
Nethack could print something like,
        "Saved level is out of date.  Renaming old save file to <file>.e."
and continue with a new game.
At the very least, it should confirm before deleting a file that
is very difficult to recreate.
This change would take place in unixmain.c and the corresponding files
for non-unix systems.

